# Random numbers: a program can draw a number it does not know in advance, so its questions are
# about what could happen, how many ways, and whether every number has the same chance. The
# observatory's astronomers play dice on a cloudy night.
lesson coding-random-numbers-and-a-fair-die v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=6 subject=coding levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Random numbers and a fair die"
  goal "Say what a program with random numbers could do, count the ways, and tell whether a die it makes is fair."

  look {
    scene 36x10 {
      program p code=["set roll to random 1 to 6", "say roll"] at=canvas(1, 0)
      die d1 face=3 right-of=p gap=2
      observatory obs open=0 right-of=d1 gap=2
    }
    say """
      Clouds cover the stars, so the astronomers at the observatory play a dice game, and the
      program is their die. Random 1 to 6 picks a whole number from 1 to 6, and nobody knows which
      until it runs. So instead of asking what it will say, ask what it could say. A die is fair
      when every number it can say has the same chance.
      """
  }
  do {
    show coding.rand-could k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show coding.rand-range k=0 m=0 easy-k=1 hard-m=1 hard-k=1
    level easy {
      worked coding.rand-range k=0 m=0 level=medium
    }
    show coding.rand-never k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show coding.rand-fair k=0 hard-k=1
    show coding.rand-ways t=4 easy-t=3 hard-t=7
    show coding.rand-fair k=2 easy-k=1
    show coding.rand-ways t=10 easy-t=11 hard-t=6
    show coding.rand-build k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show coding.rand-range k=2 m=1
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show coding.rand-spinner k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show coding.rand-likeliest k=0 easy-k=1 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "A random number could be any number in its range. A die is fair when every number it can say has the same chance."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Nobody, the program included, knows which number random 1 to 6 will give, so the questions here
    ask what could happen and in how many ways, which always has one answer. The wrong ideas to
    watch for are thinking the range leaves out its ends, and thinking every total of two dice is as
    likely as every other. In the child's working, look for lists of what could happen and tables of
    pairs rather than guesses. If the child is stuck, roll a real die, or two, and write every
    result down; after a few dozen rolls of two dice the sevens pile up. Stay another day if the
    child still says one total is lucky. Off the page, check a board game's spinner or dice for
    fairness by counting its faces or wedges. The two-star question counts the numbers that lead to
    each colour, and the three-star question is a table of all the pairs. The build question orders
    four cards so the rover stops on the flag whatever it rolls: it moves by the roll and then by
    what is left, so the child reasons about every roll rather than one.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the ranges start at 1, the least and most of two dice are found for dice of 1 to 4 and one is worked in the teacher's pen, the change after the roll is adding 3, the programs to judge are a fair one and one that turns 0 into 6, the totals to count are at the ends, where there are few ways, the spinner's fair question is the plainest, and the three-star uses dice of 1 to 4. The cards to order are for rolls from 1 to 3."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the range starts at 0, the two dice are multiplied rather than added, the change after the roll takes 1 away, the programs to judge turn 0 into 6 or 1 into 2, and neither is fair, the totals to count are 6 and 7 in the middle, where there are most ways, the spinner's fair question asks about numbers less than 4 of 6, and the three-star uses dice of 1 to 5. The cards to order take the rover 9 squares on rolls from 1 to 6."
  }
}
